const e='<h2>What the rumours said</h2>\n\n  <p>The "iPhone desktop mode" story has been running for a couple of years. The original reporting pointed at a Stage Manager-like interface for iPhones with USB-C when connected to an external display, not a full desktop, but at least a windowed environment that used the whole screen. It was widely expected to ship, and widely written up as Apple\'s answer to Samsung DeX.</p>\n\n  <p>It didn\'t ship. People later found unfinished code in iOS 26.1 that could be coaxed into showing something Stage-Manager-shaped on an external display, and that route was closed off again in the 26.2 betas. Unshipped code in a release build is not a feature; it\'s evidence someone at Apple was working on one.</p>\n\n  <h2>What you actually get today</h2>\n\n  <div class="table-scroll">\n    <table>\n      <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>External display behaviour on stock iOS/iPadOS</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>iPhone (any model)</td><td>Mirroring only. Some apps, video players, Keynote, take over the screen themselves.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>iPad without M-series chip</td><td>Mirroring only.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>iPad with M-series chip</td><td>Stage Manager on an external display: real extended desktop, windows, the lot.</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>So the answer to "does my iPhone have a desktop mode" is no, and the answer to "will an iPad give me one" is only if you bought an expensive one.</p>\n\n  <h2>Why mirroring looks so bad on a monitor</h2>\n\n  <p>It\'s a shape problem. An iPhone screen is roughly 19.5:9. A monitor or TV is 16:9. Mirroring sends the display a copy of a tall, narrow picture, and the display pads the sides with black to make it fit. You end up using maybe 60% of the panel you paid for, and everything on it is scaled for a hand rather than a room.</p>\n\n  <p>No setting in iOS fixes this, because iOS isn\'t drawing anything for the monitor, it\'s copying what the phone is drawing for itself.</p>\n\n  <h2>What to use instead</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> takes the other approach. Instead of mirroring, it renders its own desktop at the display\'s own resolution and drives the external screen directly. You get windows you can move and resize, a dock, a file manager, a real desktop browser, media players and document viewers, on any monitor, TV or projector, from any iPhone or iPad that can output video at all.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/desktop-ipados.webp" alt="A real webpage about iPadOS open in a full-width browser window on an external display." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Not a mirror, a desktop rendered at the display\'s own resolution.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Because it draws its own environment, it doesn\'t depend on Stage Manager, on an M-series chip, or on Apple shipping anything. An iPad that Apple never gave external display support to gets a full desktop; so does an iPhone.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/desktop-multitasking.webp" alt="Three overlapping windows, a file manager, notes and a browser, on one external display." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Real windows, several at a time.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>What it can do</h2>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>A desktop browser</strong>, tabs, bookmarks bar, downloads, password manager, reader mode, and an ad blocker that\'s on by default and handles YouTube video ads.</li>\n    <li><strong>Full-screen everything</strong>, the green window button removes all interface, so video and slides fill the panel edge to edge. <a href="/guides/youtube-fullscreen-on-tv">Watching YouTube full screen</a> is one of the most common reasons people install it.</li>\n    <li><strong>A real file manager</strong> that can <a href="/guides/mount-icloud-local-and-external-drives">mount iCloud Drive folders, folders from your phone, and external USB drives</a>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Document viewers</strong>, a proper PDF viewer with annotation, plus Word, Excel and PowerPoint preview.</li>\n    <li><strong>A Photos app</strong> reading your real library, with a configurable full-screen slideshow.</li>\n    <li><strong>Installable websites</strong>, put Gmail, Sheets or YouTube in the dock as their own app.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mouse and keyboard support</strong>, or your phone\'s screen as a trackpad if you have neither.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2>The honest limitations</h2>\n\n  <p>Two things are worth saying plainly, because articles that pretend otherwise waste your time.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>It\'s a desktop environment, not iOS on a monitor.</strong> Your other iPhone apps don\'t run inside it. What runs is the browser, the built-in apps, and any website you install, which covers a very large share of what people actually do at a computer, but it isn\'t Instagram on a TV.</p>\n\n  <p><strong>Some embedded page elements resist a click from the external display.</strong> Every iOS browser must use Apple\'s WebKit engine, and cross-domain frames, consent banners, some sign-in boxes, payment fields, are deliberately hardened against programmatic input as an anti-fraud measure. That protection also catches a cursor coming from a second screen. XeOS handles this with a button that hands the page to your phone\'s screen for the one tap, then sends it straight back. It\'s explained fully in <a href="/guides/device-layout-mode">the Device Layout Mode guide</a>.</p>\n\n  <h2>Will Apple ever ship this?</h2>\n\n  <p>Probably, eventually, in some form, the code exists and the demand is obvious. But "eventually" has already meant more than one release cycle, it will almost certainly be limited to recent USB-C hardware, and it will be a windowing mode rather than a full desktop. If you have a monitor and a phone today, waiting isn\'t the strongest plan.</p>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, TV or projector, no Stage Manager, no M-series chip, no waiting for iOS.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n\n    <h3>Is there a desktop mode in iOS 26?</h3>\n    <p>No. Unfinished code was found in iOS 26.1 and closed off in 26.2 betas, but no shipping desktop mode exists for iPhone.</p>\n\n    <h3>Does the iPhone have anything like Samsung DeX?</h3>\n    <p>Not from Apple. Third-party apps are the only route today.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can any iPad extend rather than mirror?</h3>\n    <p>Only M-series iPads, through Stage Manager. Everything else mirrors.</p>\n\n    <h3>Do I need a special cable?</h3>\n    <p>USB-C to HDMI for USB-C devices, a Lightning Digital AV Adapter for Lightning iPhones. AirPlay also works.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
//# sourceMappingURL=did-ios-26-add-desktop-mode-BJLMok5f.js.map
